exec(open('c2.py').read())
# ---------------- reproductive
case('em-ectopic','Lower abdominal pain, missed period and collapse','reproductive','reproductive',
 'Explain why an ectopic pregnancy usually occurs in the uterine tube and why rupture causes shoulder-tip pain and shock.',
 'A 27-year-old woman with a missed period has sudden severe pain in the lower abdomen, vaginal bleeding, and then feels faint and collapses. She also has pain at the tip of the right shoulder.',
 ['uterine-tube','ovary','uterus','fimbriae-of-uterine-tube'],
 'Fertilisation takes place in the ampulla of the uterine tube. If the embryo implants in the tube (most often in the ampulla) rather than in the uterus, the thin tubal wall cannot stretch and may rupture, bleeding into the peritoneal cavity. Blood irritating the diaphragm is felt as shoulder-tip pain through the phrenic nerve (C3-C5). Blood supply to the tube comes from the ovarian and uterine arteries, which can bleed heavily.',
 ['Call the emergency number. Any woman of child-bearing age with severe lower abdominal pain, faintness and a possible pregnancy needs emergency care.','Lay her down and raise the legs if she is faint. Keep her warm.','Give nothing to eat or drink in case surgery is needed.','Note the time of the last period, bleeding and symptoms. Do not give medicines unless a doctor advises.','In hospital: a pregnancy test, ultrasound, blood tests, and medicines or surgery (usually laparoscopy).'],
 'Collapse, severe pain or heavy bleeding in early pregnancy is a life-threatening emergency.',
 [mc('Where does fertilisation normally take place?',['Ampulla of the uterine tube','Body of the uterus','Cervix','Surface of the ovary'],0,'Fertilisation occurs in the ampulla of the uterine tube.','foundation','Locate fertilisation and ectopic pregnancy'),
  mc('Which arteries supply the uterine tube?',['Ovarian and uterine arteries','Inferior mesenteric and sigmoid arteries','Internal pudendal and inferior rectal arteries','Superior vesical arteries only'],0,'The tube is supplied by tubal branches of the ovarian and uterine arteries which anastomose in the mesosalpinx.','intermediate','Name the blood supply of the uterine tube')])
case('em-testicular-torsion','Sudden severe pain in the scrotum','reproductive','reproductive',
 'Explain how twisting of the spermatic cord cuts off the blood supply of the testis and why time matters.',
 'A 14-year-old wakes at night with sudden, severe pain in the left testis and lower abdomen. The testis is high, swollen, very tender and he has vomited.',
 ['testis','spermatic-cord','scrotum','epididymis'],
 'The testicular artery, the pampiniform plexus of veins and the ductus deferens run in the spermatic cord. If the testis twists on the cord (often due to a high attachment of the tunica vaginalis, the bell-clapper deformity), the veins are blocked first and then the artery, so the testis becomes ischaemic. Pain is also felt in the lower abdomen because testicular afferents enter the cord at T10. The cremasteric reflex (stroking the inner thigh makes the testis rise) is usually absent.',
 ['Go to the emergency department at once. Do not wait to see if the pain settles.','Do not give food or drink, in case of surgery.','Do not delay for scans or tests if the doctor suspects torsion: urgent surgery to untwist and fix both testes is needed, ideally within about 6 hours of the start of the pain to save the testis.','Pain relief may be given by the clinicians.'],
 'Sudden scrotal pain in a boy or man is torsion until proven otherwise.',
 [mc('Which structures are in the spermatic cord?',['Testicular artery, pampiniform plexus and ductus deferens','Femoral artery and vein','Inferior epigastric vessels only','Ureter and ovarian vein'],0,'The spermatic cord contains the ductus deferens, testicular and cremasteric arteries, pampiniform plexus, nerves and lymphatics.','foundation','List contents of the spermatic cord'),
  tf('A normal cremasteric reflex makes torsion very likely.',False,'False. The cremasteric reflex is usually absent in torsion.','intermediate','Recognise the signs of torsion')])
case('em-ovarian-torsion','Sudden one-sided pelvic pain with vomiting','reproductive','reproductive',
 'Explain how the ovarian ligaments and the ovarian vessels allow torsion of the ovary.',
 'A 24-year-old woman has sudden, severe pain in the right lower abdomen with nausea and vomiting. An ultrasound shows an enlarged right ovary.',
 ['ovary','uterine-tube','uterus'],
 'The ovary is attached to the uterus by the ovarian ligament and to the pelvic wall by the suspensory ligament, which carries the ovarian vessels. A cyst or mass makes the ovary heavy, so it can twist on this pedicle, blocking the veins and then the ovarian artery, causing pain and ischaemia. The ovarian artery arises from the abdominal aorta at L2 and the pain is referred to T10.',
 ['Go to the emergency department. This is a surgical emergency, often confused with appendicitis.','Give nothing by mouth in case surgery is needed.','Doctors confirm with ultrasound and pregnancy test and usually untwist the ovary by laparoscopy, trying to save it.'],
 'Severe sudden pelvic pain with vomiting needs urgent assessment.',
 [mc('From which vessel does the ovarian artery arise?',['Internal iliac artery','Abdominal aorta','Inferior mesenteric artery','Femoral artery'],1,'The ovarian artery arises from the abdominal aorta below the renal arteries.','foundation','Name the origin of the ovarian artery'),
  mc('Which ligament carries the ovarian vessels?',['Suspensory ligament of the ovary','Round ligament of the uterus','Cardinal ligament','Ovarian ligament'],0,'The suspensory ligament of the ovary carries the ovarian vessels, nerves and lymphatics.','intermediate','Name ovarian ligaments')])
# ---------------- endocrine
case('em-hypoglycaemia','Low blood sugar in a person with diabetes','endocrine','endocrine',
 'Relate the pancreatic islets to glucose control and describe the first-aid treatment of hypoglycaemia.',
 'A woman who takes insulin becomes sweaty, shaky and confused after skipping lunch. She then becomes drowsy.',
 ['pancreas','liver','brain'],
 'The beta cells of the pancreatic islets release insulin, which lowers blood sugar; alpha cells release glucagon, which raises it by stimulating the liver to release glucose. Too much insulin or too little food drops blood sugar. The brain depends on glucose and soon becomes confused. Adrenaline release causes the sweating, tremor and fast pulse.',
 ['If the person is awake and can swallow, give 15-20 g of fast-acting sugar: for example 150-200 ml of fruit juice or a non-diet fizzy drink, or glucose tablets or gel.','Recheck after 10-15 minutes (blood glucose if a meter is available). Repeat if still low, then give a snack of long-acting carbohydrate.','If the person is drowsy or cannot swallow, do not put food or drink in their mouth. Place them in the recovery position and call the emergency number.','If a trained person has a glucagon kit, they can give it according to the instructions.','If they become unresponsive and are not breathing normally, start CPR.'],
 'Drowsiness, a fit or unconsciousness with diabetes needs emergency help.',
 [mc('Which pancreatic cells secrete insulin?',['Alpha cells','Beta cells','Acinar cells','Delta cells'],1,'Beta cells of the islets of Langerhans secrete insulin.','foundation','Name the islet cells'),
  mc('What is the correct first action for a conscious person with symptoms of low blood sugar?',['Give a fast-acting sugar by mouth','Give an extra insulin dose','Give nothing and wait','Give large amounts of water only'],0,'Fast-acting sugar by mouth raises the blood glucose quickly.','foundation','State first-aid for hypoglycaemia')])
case('em-adrenal-crisis','Collapse in a person on long-term steroids','endocrine','endocrine',
 'Relate the adrenal cortex to cortisol production and explain adrenal crisis.',
 'A man who takes steroid tablets long-term stopped them during a stomach illness. He is now vomiting, very weak, dizzy and has a low blood pressure.',
 ['adrenal-gland','kidney','pituitary-gland'],
 'The adrenal cortex produces cortisol (under ACTH from the pituitary) and aldosterone; the medulla produces adrenaline. Long-term steroid tablets suppress the pituitary-adrenal axis, so the adrenal glands cannot respond when the body is stressed or the tablets are stopped. Without cortisol, blood pressure and blood sugar fall and the person can die. The glands are supplied by three arteries (from the inferior phrenic, aorta and renal arteries) and drained by one vein on each side.',
 ['Call the emergency number. This is life-threatening.','Lay the person down and raise the legs. Keep warm.','If the person carries an emergency hydrocortisone injection kit and someone is trained, give it as instructed.','Do not give food or drink if vomiting or drowsy.','In hospital: intravenous hydrocortisone, fluids and glucose.'],
 'Collapse or severe illness in anyone with known adrenal disease or long-term steroid use needs emergency help.',
 [mc('Which hormone from the adrenal cortex is lacking in an adrenal crisis?',['Cortisol','Adrenaline','Insulin','Thyroxine'],0,'Cortisol (with aldosterone) is deficient in adrenal crisis.','foundation','Name adrenal hormones'),
  mc('Which gland controls cortisol release through ACTH?',['Pituitary gland','Thyroid gland','Parathyroid glands','Pineal gland'],0,'ACTH from the anterior pituitary stimulates cortisol production.','foundation','Link the pituitary and adrenal glands')])
case('em-neck-haematoma','Neck swelling and noisy breathing after thyroid surgery','endocrine','endocrine',
 'Relate the anatomy of the neck and the thyroid to the emergency caused by a neck haematoma after surgery.',
 'A woman is recovering after a thyroidectomy. Within hours, her neck swells, she has a tight feeling and a noisy, difficult breathing pattern.',
 ['thyroid-gland','trachea','parathyroid-glands','larynx'],
 'The thyroid is wrapped by the pretracheal fascia. Bleeding from the superior or inferior thyroid arteries or their branches collects in this closed space, compressing the trachea and larynx, and the swelling also causes venous and lymphatic congestion of the larynx. The recurrent laryngeal nerves run in the tracheo-oesophageal grooves behind the gland, and the parathyroid glands sit on its posterior surface.',
 ['Call for senior surgical and anaesthetic help immediately: this is a threat to the airway.','Sit the person upright and give oxygen.','Trained staff open the neck wound at the bedside (cut the skin sutures and the strap muscle layers) to release the blood, before any attempt to intubate.','Return to theatre to find and stop the bleeding.','Afterwards check the voice (recurrent laryngeal nerve) and calcium level (parathyroid glands).'],
 'Neck swelling or breathing difficulty after neck surgery is an immediate emergency.',
 [mc('Which structures lie directly behind the thyroid gland and are at risk in surgery?',['Recurrent laryngeal nerve and parathyroid glands','Phrenic nerve and thoracic duct','Hypoglossal nerve and carotid sinus','Vagus nerve only'],0,'The recurrent laryngeal nerves and the parathyroid glands are at the back of the gland.','intermediate','Name structures at risk in thyroid surgery'),
  mc('Which arteries supply the thyroid gland?',['Superior and inferior thyroid arteries','Facial and lingual arteries','Vertebral and basilar arteries','Subscapular arteries'],0,'The superior thyroid artery (external carotid) and the inferior thyroid artery (thyrocervical trunk) supply the gland.','foundation','Name thyroid arteries')])
# ---------------- nervous
case('em-stroke','Sudden facial droop and arm weakness','nervous','nervousAnatomy',
 'Relate the arteries of the brain to the signs of stroke and describe the FAST response.',
 'A 70-year-old woman suddenly cannot raise her right arm, her right face droops and her speech is slurred.',
 ['middle-cerebral-artery','cerebral-arterial-circle','internal-carotid-artery','cerebrum'],
 'The middle cerebral artery, a branch of the internal carotid artery, supplies the lateral surface of the cerebral hemisphere, including the motor and sensory areas of the face and arm and, on the dominant side, the speech areas. A blocked artery causes weakness of the opposite face and arm and speech difficulty. The anterior cerebral artery supplies the leg area on the medial surface; the posterior cerebral artery supplies the occipital lobe and vision.',
 ['Use FAST: Face (droop), Arm (weakness), Speech (slurred) and Time to call the emergency number immediately.','Note the exact time the symptoms began (or when the person was last seen well). This decides whether clot-dissolving treatment or clot retrieval can be given.','Keep the person safe, lying with the head slightly raised, and loosen tight clothing. Do not give food, drink or tablets. Aspirin is not given at home in case the stroke is caused by bleeding.','If unresponsive and breathing, place in the recovery position. If not breathing normally, start CPR.','In hospital: urgent CT or MRI, clot-dissolving drugs or thrombectomy for blocked arteries, and care for haemorrhage.'],
 'Any sudden face droop, arm weakness or speech change needs an emergency call, even if it settles within minutes.',
 [mc('Which artery supplies the lateral surface of the cerebral hemisphere, including the face and arm motor areas?',['Anterior cerebral artery','Middle cerebral artery','Posterior cerebral artery','Basilar artery'],1,'The middle cerebral artery supplies the lateral hemisphere including the face and arm areas of the motor cortex.','foundation','Name the arterial territories of the brain'),
  mc('What does the F in FAST stand for?',['Fever','Face','Fall','Fatigue'],1,'Face droop; the other letters are Arm, Speech and Time.','foundation','Recall FAST')])
case('em-extradural','Head injury, a lucid interval and then drowsiness','nervous','nervousAnatomy',
 'Explain why a blow to the side of the head can cause an extradural haematoma after a lucid interval.',
 'A boy is hit on the side of the head by a ball. He is briefly knocked out, wakes and seems well for two hours, then becomes drowsy, has a severe headache and vomits.',
 ['dura-mater','brain','cerebrum'],
 'The thin pterion region on the side of the skull, where the frontal, parietal, temporal and sphenoid bones meet, overlies the anterior branch of the middle meningeal artery. A fracture can tear this artery, and arterial blood builds up between the skull and the dura. Pressure inside the skull rises, and the person deteriorates after a lucid interval. The brain can herniate under the tentorium and compress the oculomotor nerve, causing a dilated pupil on the same side.',
 ['Call the emergency number for any head injury with loss of consciousness, vomiting, severe headache, drowsiness, fits or a clear fluid or blood from the nose or ears.','Keep the person still and lying with the head slightly raised. Support the neck if a neck injury is possible.','Do not give food or drink. Watch breathing and responsiveness; place in the recovery position if unconscious and breathing.','Control bleeding from scalp wounds with gentle pressure, avoiding pressure on a depressed fracture.','In hospital: CT scan and, if there is a haematoma, surgery to remove it and control the artery.'],
 'A lucid interval does not mean a head injury is safe: later deterioration needs emergency care.',
 [mc('Which artery is commonly torn in an extradural haematoma?',['Middle meningeal artery','Anterior cerebral artery','Basilar artery','Vertebral artery'],0,'The anterior branch of the middle meningeal artery lies under the pterion.','foundation','Name the artery of extradural haematoma'),
  mc('Which cranial nerve is often compressed when the brain herniates, causing a dilated pupil?',['Oculomotor nerve (III)','Optic nerve (II)','Trochlear nerve (IV)','Abducens nerve (VI)'],0,'The oculomotor nerve, which carries the parasympathetic constriction fibres, is compressed by the herniating uncus.','advanced','Link herniation and the oculomotor nerve')])
case('em-spinal-injury','Suspected spinal injury after a fall','nervous','nervousAnatomy',
 'Relate the level of a spinal cord injury to the loss of function and describe first aid for suspected spinal injury.',
 'A man falls from a ladder and complains of neck pain. He cannot feel or move his legs, and his breathing is shallow.',
 ['spinal-cord','vertebral-column','phrenic-nerve','diaphragm'],
 'The spinal cord lies in the vertebral canal. An injury in the cervical region can paralyse all four limbs. The phrenic nerve arises from C3, C4 and C5 ("C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive"), so an injury above C5 can stop the diaphragm. Spinal shock causes low blood pressure and a slow pulse because the sympathetic outflow (T1-L2) is lost.',
 ['Call the emergency number. Do not move the person unless there is immediate danger (fire, traffic) or they stop breathing.','Tell the person to stay still. Ask a helper to hold the head in line with the body with both hands on either side, without pulling.','Do not remove a helmet unless it blocks the airway and a trained person is present.','If the person must be turned (vomiting, or to start CPR), roll them as one unit with the head, neck and trunk kept in line.','Watch breathing and keep the person warm. If they are not breathing normally, open the airway (jaw thrust if trained) and start CPR.'],
 'Any fall from height, diving accident or high-speed crash with neck or back pain, numbness or weakness is an emergency.',
 [mc('Which spinal nerve roots form the phrenic nerve?',['C3-C5','T1-T4','L1-L3','S2-S4'],0,'The phrenic nerve arises from C3, C4 and C5.','foundation','Name the phrenic nerve roots'),
  tf('A conscious person with a suspected neck injury should be encouraged to sit up and move the neck to test it.',False,'False. They should stay still with the head held in line.','foundation','Apply the first aid for spinal injury')])
case('em-seizure','A person having a convulsive seizure','nervous','nervousAnatomy',
 'Relate abnormal electrical activity of the cerebral cortex to the features of a seizure and describe first aid.',
 'A woman falls to the ground, stiffens, then jerks all four limbs for about 90 seconds. Her lips look blue and she is drooling.',
 ['cerebrum','brain','hippocampus'],
 'A seizure is a sudden, abnormal discharge of cortical neurones. In a generalised convulsive seizure, the whole cortex is involved, causing loss of awareness, stiffening and jerking. Seizures that start in a focal area, for example the temporal lobe including the hippocampus, may start with a strange feeling or smell. Afterwards the person is drowsy and confused (the postictal state).',
 ['Stay calm and note the time the seizure starts. Protect the person from injury: clear the space and cushion the head.','Do not hold the person down and do not put anything in the mouth.','When the jerking stops, check breathing and place the person in the recovery position. Stay with them until fully alert.','Call the emergency number if the seizure lasts more than 5 minutes, one seizure follows another, the person is injured, is pregnant or has diabetes, or does not wake up.','If the person is not breathing normally after the seizure, start CPR.'],
 'A seizure lasting longer than 5 minutes, repeated seizures, or a first seizure needs emergency care.',
 [mc('What should you NOT do during a convulsive seizure?',['Cushion the head','Note the time','Put something in the mouth','Move hazards away'],2,'Nothing should be put in the mouth; it can cause injury and block the airway.','foundation','State seizure first aid'),
  mc('After the jerking stops, what position is used?',['Recovery position','Standing','Face down flat','Sitting leaning forward'],0,'The recovery position keeps the airway open and allows fluid to drain.','foundation','Use the recovery position')])
